  <!-- ===== THE SHARP METHOD ===== -->
  <section class="prog-section" id="approach">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="eyebrow">The SHARP Method</p>
      <h2 class="prog-section__heading">How the SHARP Method works in General Paper</h2>
      <p class="prog-section__sub">Five steps, taken from the way lawyers read and argue a case, and applied to GP essays and comprehension.</p>

      <div class="card-grid card-grid--2">
        <div class="prog-card">
          <h3 class="prog-card__title">See</h3>
          <p class="prog-card__desc">A misread question costs many GP marks. At the See step your child reads each question the way a lawyer reads a brief. What is the command word? (&lsquo;Discuss&rsquo; wants balance, &lsquo;evaluate&rsquo; wants judgement, &lsquo;assess&rsquo; wants criteria.) How wide is the claim: all societies, just Singapore, only the last decade? Which counter-position does the examiner expect? In comprehension, the See step means telling literal, inference, vocabulary and author&rsquo;s-craft questions apart. For the AQ, it means mapping each author&rsquo;s main claim before deciding to agree, disagree or qualify.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="prog-card">
          <h3 class="prog-card__title">Hit</h3>
          <p class="prog-card__desc">Once the question type is clear, your child picks the framework built for it. Essays use P-E-E-L (Point, Evidence, Explanation, Link): thesis-driven for argumentative, balanced for discursive, criteria-led for evaluative. Comprehension questions use Quote + Explain, summary uses the Content Point List + Paraphrase Chain, and the AQ uses the AQ Response Template. Each one follows what the 8881 mark scheme rewards.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="prog-card">
          <h3 class="prog-card__title">Apply</h3>
          <p class="prog-card__desc">Your child doesn&rsquo;t write a whole essay from scratch. They build one P-E-E-L paragraph with guidance, because one paragraph done properly teaches more than five done carelessly. Jeremy puts up a real Cambridge past-paper question, students write in a shared document, and he annotates as they go. For the AQ, students map the author&rsquo;s claims, find the Singapore angle and argue from their own knowledge, not stock phrases. The feedback is specific: &lsquo;Your topic sentence restates the question instead of stating your position.&rsquo; &lsquo;This example is too vague: name the country, the year and the outcome.&rsquo;</p>
        </div>

        <div class="prog-card">
          <h3 class="prog-card__title">Refine &amp; Practise</h3>
          <p class="prog-card__desc">Your child checks every paragraph against our GP checklist. Is the thesis in the first sentence, not buried in paragraph three? Does each example name a country, date or outcome? Is the counter-argument answered, or just mentioned? Is the conclusion a weighed judgement, or fence-sitting? Then they rewrite the paragraph from memory until P-E-E-L is automatic under time pressure. Their argument bank, a running file of examples by theme with evidence paragraphs already written, grows every week.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ===== QUESTION-TYPE FRAMEWORKS ===== -->
  <section class="prog-section" id="frameworks">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="eyebrow">Question-type frameworks</p>
      <h2 class="prog-section__heading">A framework for every question type in the GP paper</h2>
      <p class="prog-section__sub">Students say GP is impossible to study for because &lsquo;there&rsquo;s no syllabus&rsquo;. That&rsquo;s half true. There&rsquo;s no content syllabus, but there is a skills syllabus: each question type tests one skill, and each skill has a framework your child can practise until it holds up in the exam.</p>

      <div class="card-grid card-grid--2">
        <div class="prog-card">
          <h3 class="prog-card__title">Argumentative essay: thesis-driven P-E-E-L</h3>
          <p class="prog-card__desc">An argumentative essay needs one clear position, defended with evidence and reasoning. Your child states the thesis in the introduction, answering the question directly, instead of opening with &lsquo;this essay will discuss&rsquo;. Each body paragraph follows P-E-E-L: a Point that moves the thesis forward, Evidence from a specific real example (named country, policy, outcome), an Explanation of why the evidence proves the point, and a Link back to the thesis. The second-last paragraph takes on the strongest opposing view and rebuts it. This is what the Band 4 descriptors call &lsquo;sustained and well-supported&rsquo;.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="prog-card">
          <h3 class="prog-card__title">Discursive essay: balanced P-E-E-L</h3>
          <p class="prog-card__desc">Discursive questions (&lsquo;To what extent&hellip;&rsquo;, &lsquo;How far do you agree&hellip;&rsquo;) set up a tension that needs balance, not fence-sitting. Your child writes two P-E-E-L paragraphs for one side, then concedes before giving two equally strong paragraphs for the other. Most students squeeze the other view into one sentence; we teach your child to concede its strongest point, then explain why their own view still carries more weight. The conclusion weighs both and reaches a verdict, rather than settling for &lsquo;both sides have merit&rsquo;.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="prog-card">
          <h3 class="prog-card__title">Evaluative essay: criteria-led P-E-E-L</h3>
          <p class="prog-card__desc">Evaluative questions ask for a judgement (&lsquo;Evaluate the effectiveness&hellip;&rsquo;, &lsquo;How successful&hellip;&rsquo;). Most students treat them as argumentative questions and list pros and cons. Instead, your child names the standard first (effectiveness? equity? sustainability?) and builds each paragraph around it. &lsquo;Has Singapore&rsquo;s education system been effective?&rsquo; becomes &lsquo;effective at what: academic results, social mobility or all-round development?&rsquo; Each criterion gets one P-E-E-L paragraph, with evidence measured against it. The conclusion gives a verdict and states its conditions, not a vague &lsquo;it depends&rsquo;.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="prog-card">
          <h3 class="prog-card__title">SAQ comprehension: Quote + Explain</h3>
          <p class="prog-card__desc">Short-answer questions reward precision, not volume. Quote + Explain has two moves: quote the phrase that holds the answer, then explain what it means in context, in your child&rsquo;s own words without losing any meaning. Inference questions need more than the surface meaning. A vocabulary question wants the word&rsquo;s meaning in this passage, not its dictionary definition. And a 2-mark question needs two distinct ideas, not one idea said twice.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <details class="prog-more">
        <summary class="btn btn--sm prog-more__toggle"><span class="prog-more__show">Show all 6 frameworks</span><span class="prog-more__hide">Hide the other 2 frameworks</span></summary>
        <div class="card-grid card-grid--2">

        <div class="prog-card">
          <h3 class="prog-card__title">Summary: Content Point List + Paraphrase Chain</h3>
          <p class="prog-card__desc">The summary question gives your child a passage and a word limit, and students lose most marks by lifting phrases or running over the limit. Our method has three steps. First, number every content point the examiner could credit (usually 8&ndash;12). Next, paraphrase each one by restructuring the sentence, not by swapping in synonyms. Then chain the points into compound sentences so they fit the limit without losing any. Students do timed summaries through the term and track their word count, content points hit and paraphrase quality.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="prog-card">
          <h3 class="prog-card__title">Application Question: AQ Response Template</h3>
          <p class="prog-card__desc">The AQ is worth 12 marks, and it&rsquo;s where many GP students lose the most, because they don&rsquo;t know what &lsquo;apply&rsquo; means here. The AQ Response Template has four moves: state each author&rsquo;s main claim in one precise sentence; say whether you agree, disagree or agree with qualifications; apply the claim to Singapore with evidence; then add your own view, drawing on knowledge the authors don&rsquo;t cover. The AQ rewards students who think beyond the passage rather than summarising it. Students keep a Singapore Context File and update it with the news every week.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      </details>
    </div>
  </section>

