  <!-- ===== BEFORE / AFTER ===== -->
  <section class="prog-section ba-section" id="before-after">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="eyebrow">Before &amp; after</p>
      <h2 class="prog-section__heading">The same point, before and after the SHARP Method</h2>
      <p class="prog-section__sub">What changes when a student stops asserting and starts arguing, shown on one body-paragraph point.</p>

      <div class="ba-question">
        <span class="ba-q-label">Essay &middot; Body paragraph &middot; &lsquo;Has technology made us less human?&rsquo;</span>
        <p>The point the student wants to make: <em>technology weakens genuine human connection.</em></p>
      </div>

      <div class="ba-grid">
        <div class="ba-card ba-card--before">
          <span class="ba-tag ba-tag--before">Before</span>
          <p class="ba-answer">&ldquo;Technology has made us less human because we are always on our phones and don&rsquo;t talk to each other any more.&rdquo;</p>
          <ul class="ba-notes">
            <li>Assertion with no evidence or example</li>
            <li>One-sided: no counter-argument</li>
            <li>Never explains <em>why</em>, or how far it&rsquo;s true</li>
          </ul>
          <span class="ba-mark ba-mark--before">Band 1&ndash;2</span>
        </div>

        <div class="ba-card ba-card--after">
          <span class="ba-tag ba-tag--after">After SHARP</span>
          <p class="ba-answer">&ldquo;Technology can erode genuine connection: many people now spend hours each day on their devices, and research links heavy social-media use to rising loneliness. Yet the same tools reconnect families across continents &mdash; so the dehumanising effect is real, but depends on how we use technology rather than on the technology itself.&rdquo;</p>
          <ul class="ba-notes">
            <li><strong>Point + Evidence</strong>: a concrete, relevant example</li>
            <li><strong>Explanation</strong> of the underlying mechanism</li>
            <li><strong>Evaluation</strong>: a counter-balance and a qualified stance</li>
          </ul>
          <span class="ba-mark ba-mark--after">Band 3</span>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p class="ba-footnote">An illustrative teaching example, not a real student&rsquo;s script. Building a point as Point&ndash;Evidence&ndash;Explanation&ndash;Link, with evaluation built in, is the <strong>P-E-E-L</strong> framework we drill at the <strong>Hit</strong> step of the SHARP Method.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ===== INSIDE A LESSON ===== -->
  <section class="prog-section lesson-flow" id="lesson-flow">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="eyebrow">Inside a lesson</p>
      <h2 class="prog-section__heading">What a 90-minute GP lesson looks like</h2>
      <p class="prog-section__intro">Many JC students start GP unsure what they&rsquo;re meant to <em>do</em> in it. Here&rsquo;s how a typical lesson runs, from the opening news brief to the work set before students log off.</p>

      <ol class="lesson-timeline">
        <li class="lesson-step">
          <span class="lesson-step__num" aria-hidden="true">01</span>
          <div class="lesson-step__body">
            <span class="lesson-step__time">0 &ndash; 5 min &middot; This week in the news</span>
            <h3 class="lesson-step__title">Three stories, three themes</h3>
            <p class="lesson-step__desc">We open with three news stories from the past week: one science and tech, one society, one geopolitics. Students name the GP theme each belongs to, so by minute five everyone has three fresh examples for this week&rsquo;s essay.</p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="lesson-step">
          <span class="lesson-step__num" aria-hidden="true">02</span>
          <div class="lesson-step__body">
            <span class="lesson-step__time">5 &ndash; 20 min &middot; Question deconstruction</span>
            <h3 class="lesson-step__title">Find the counter-claim the examiner expects</h3>
            <p class="lesson-step__desc">A real Cambridge past-paper question goes on screen. We work through the <em>See</em> step: the command word, the scope and the counter-claim to address. Then the <em>Hit</em> step: which framework fits this question? Thesis-driven P-E-E-L, criteria-led P-E-E-L or the AQ Response Template?</p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="lesson-step">
          <span class="lesson-step__num" aria-hidden="true">03</span>
          <div class="lesson-step__body">
            <span class="lesson-step__time">20 &ndash; 50 min &middot; Live P-E-E-L drill</span>
            <h3 class="lesson-step__title">One strong paragraph, not a rushed essay</h3>
            <p class="lesson-step__desc">Students write one full P-E-E-L paragraph in real time. Jeremy moves between their answers in the shared document and flags weak topic sentences, missing evidence and lazy links as they happen. The aim is a paragraph the student can reproduce under exam pressure.</p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="lesson-step">
          <span class="lesson-step__num" aria-hidden="true">04</span>
          <div class="lesson-step__body">
            <span class="lesson-step__time">50 &ndash; 70 min &middot; Counter-argument workshop</span>
            <h3 class="lesson-step__title">Where Band 3 becomes Band 4</h3>
            <p class="lesson-step__desc">Students pair up and attack each other&rsquo;s arguments. Seeing a counter-claim coming and rebutting it is much of what separates a Band 3 essay from a Band 4 one. Every student leaves with a stronger argument than they came in with.</p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="lesson-step">
          <span class="lesson-step__num" aria-hidden="true">05</span>
          <div class="lesson-step__body">
            <span class="lesson-step__time">70 &ndash; 85 min &middot; Examiner&rsquo;s eye</span>
            <h3 class="lesson-step__title">Mark like an examiner; write like one</h3>
            <p class="lesson-step__desc">We put up two unseen sample essays, one Band 2 and one Band 4, and students work out what separates them. Once they can mark like an examiner, they start writing for one.</p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="lesson-step">
          <span class="lesson-step__num" aria-hidden="true">06</span>
          <div class="lesson-step__body">
            <span class="lesson-step__time">85 &ndash; 90 min &middot; Argument bank</span>
            <h3 class="lesson-step__title">One plan, one AQ, by Friday</h3>
            <p class="lesson-step__desc">We close by setting one essay plan and one AQ, both timed and due by Friday, so the written feedback is back before the next lesson. The week&rsquo;s news examples go into the argument bank each student keeps for the year.</p>
          </div>
        </li>
      </ol>

      <p class="lesson-flow__note">Every lesson is recorded. If your child misses one, the recording, worksheet and homework reach them within 24 hours. Between lessons, your child can send questions on WhatsApp.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

